WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.650
- [Host] Airplane seats today

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.380
are a lot smaller than they used to be,

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:05.970
but size isn't the only thing

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:07.620
that makes a chair comfortable.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:08.940
- They're just seats.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.250
They don't have the
like dynamic properties

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.300
of an ergonomic seat.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.040
- [Host] So we asked a professional.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.480
- My name is Carla Jaspers.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.610
- [Host] How she'd
redesign the airplane seat

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.430
to make it more ergonomically perfect.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.310
- For starters, I think there's
definitely an opportunity

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.800
for a bit of subtle adjustability.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.260
- [Host] Specifically,
with the lumbar support.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.840
- [Carla] Good lumbar support
could look like subtle,

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.500
yet movable lumbar support
that could be tailored

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.030
to the user's needs.

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:37.470
- [Host] Look at how flat the seatback

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:38.790
of this plane chair is.

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:42.090
Now, compare that with this
ergonomic office chair.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.030
- The more points of
contact your back can have

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.880
with a contoured and supportive
seatback, the better.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.670
The spine is sort of this natural S shape,

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.590
a very like subtle S-shaped curve.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.450
You have the cervical, thoracic,

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.430
and lumbar curvatures of the spine.

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.470
- [Host] The lumbar support
should hit you right

00:00:58.470 --> 00:00:59.790
in the small of your back,

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:01.530
which is why adjustability helps

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.600
when catering to various sitters.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.720
- I would also advocate
for something comfortable

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.760
around the head and neck region.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.280
Adjusting up and down, that's a start.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.040
I would say ideal state, it would be great

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.650
if there was something that
was just a bit more contoured

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:17.850
to support the head.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.400
If you think about a child's
car seat, for example,

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.070
there's a contoured head and neck support

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:27.510
to not only provide that
comfort in different positions,

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:31.320
but also as a safety factor.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.360
- [Host] Then there's the seat pan.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.010
- If the seat pan could be adjustable,

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:38.280
that would accommodate
people of varied leg lengths.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.150
At very least, you could
reexamine sizing of the seats.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:43.980
- [Host] Some planes
have decreased seat width

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:47.400
from 18" to 17 to fit in more passengers.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.150
And in some cases, seats
can be as narrow as 16".

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.400
Compare that with this
ergonomic desk chair,

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.420
with seat width ranging from 19 to 21.6".

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:58.800
- With regards to seat width,

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.670
I think 18 to 19 feels more
comfortable for most people.

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:05.160
- If passengers could adjust
the depth of their seat pan,

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:06.780
it would help them support their knees

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:09.360
and be able to sit fully
against the seatback.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.280
- So if you have somebody
with longer legs,

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.800
they would get that much more
leg and postural support.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.010
And for the person who found
the seat pan to be too deep,

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.050
if they could bring the depth back,

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.520
it could mitigate contact pressure

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.200
or discomfort for them as well.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.390
The depth is important
in terms of supporting

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.460
not only the lower extremities,
but also the spine.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.140
If the seat pan is too deep

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.540
or compresses the back
of the knees or the legs,

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:33.360
it's great to have some sort
of support under the feet.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.310
- [Host] Typical ergonomic
chairs allow their heights

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:38.580
to be adjusted so your feet
can sit firmly on the ground.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.420
On an airplane, a fold out
footrest solves this problem.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.930
- A metal bar or just some
sort of component under there

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:47.640
that allows people to sort
of anchor their feet in

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.740
for postural support,
which like allows them

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.780
to then shift back into their seat.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.000
- [Host] Premium seats can
come with those footrests,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.640
but economy seats typically don't.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.100
They're especially important
for shorter passengers

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.840
to support themselves properly.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:02.970
- But for somebody like me,
I don't necessarily want that

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.790
so it'd be nice if it went
away depending on the user.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.860
- [Host] This brings us
to the biggest hurdle.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.780
- Leg room is paramount.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.500
I think that's like really
one of the main needs

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.020
for redesign on airplanes.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:17.670
- [Host] The obvious way to add leg room

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:20.220
is moving seat rows farther apart,

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:21.780
increasing the seat pitch.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.670
The average seat pitch
between airplane seats

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.930
has decreased from around 34 to 36"

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:29.550
to 32 over the past few decades.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.410
Advocacy group FlyersRights says

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.680
that in some planes, it's down to 28".

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.190
- When you start to shave
inches off that distance,

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:39.510
it compromises leg room.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.310
And this is when you run into issues

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.990
with people's legs being unable to fit

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:45.900
when they're sitting in their seats.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.430
I think anything less than 32,

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:50.520
people start to notice significantly
that seat is too small.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:53.220
Over 32, people find
it to be more spacious.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:54.977
With regards to the
materials that are used,

00:03:54.977 --> 00:03:58.698
Tempur-Pedic foams or
memory foams come to mind.

00:03:58.698 --> 00:04:00.480
Something that would keep its shape

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.880
and really offer that contoured support.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:06.660
Because these arm rests are
predominantly hard plastics

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:09.060
or metal materials, there's
definitely an opportunity

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:11.280
to introduce some softer materials

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.440
to mitigate contact pressure.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:14.730
- [Host] Airplane seats have gotten

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:17.610
significantly more
lightweight in recent decades.

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:18.900
And industry experts say

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.930
it's difficult to simultaneously
optimize for durability,

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:23.730
cost, and comfort.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.760
But for ergonomics, cushioning is key.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.910
- It's been successful to
think about foam or leather

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:31.620
on other types of seating.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:33.750
It would just have to be a material

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:36.630
that could endure high volume of use.

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:38.010
On a scale of one to 10,

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:41.760
economy airline seats would probably fall

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:43.620
around a three or a four

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:46.710
with regards to being
ergonomically designed.

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:48.930
- [Host] Jasper's
recommendations are based solely

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:51.150
on her expertise in ergonomics.

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:55.710
Not necessarily engineering,
safety, cost, and longevity.

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:57.870
- It's a very technical area of design.

00:04:57.870 --> 00:04:59.970
There's obviously the
constraints of weight.

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:03.840
It's a very engineering-heavy
design challenge for sure.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.120
The opportunity for design really stems

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:09.150
from not only giving a
little bit more space

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:10.895
back to the passenger,

00:05:10.895 --> 00:05:15.060
but perhaps also adding a bit more support

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:19.050
to the components of the
seat to provide support

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:20.850
for a range of passengers.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.040
Being a woman who is six feet tall,

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:26.250
my knees are always up against
the seat in front of me.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.980
The seats are narrow on
my shoulders and hips.

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:31.500
The headrest, it either sort
of pushes my head forward

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:33.480
or just sort of misses
the mark altogether.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:37.710
I find myself sort of trying
to collapse into the seat

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:39.990
and not disrupt my seatmates,

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:43.590
but at the expense of my ability
to move or be comfortable.

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:48.457
It's kind of become this
understood experience,

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:52.560
where if you're flying economy,

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:56.400
you have sort of made peace with the idea

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:57.810
that it's probably not going to be

00:05:57.810 --> 00:05:59.430
a very comfortable experience.

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:01.323
And are we okay with that?

